        <mxCell id="cap" value="Figure 1. Argo Workflows, from submit to pod and back. (1) The CLI reaches the cluster through pkg/apiclient, which speaks to argo-server, or&#10;straight to the Kubernetes API, so the controller also runs stand alone. (2) Informers on Workflow, Pod and WorkflowTaskResult all push the&#10;same key, namespace/name, onto wfQueue, and a worker holds workflowKeyLock while it reconciles that key, so one workflow is never&#10;reconciled twice at the same time. (3) operate() works on a DeepCopy of the informer object: it merges task results, reconciles pods, then&#10;walks the templates from spec.entrypoint, and gives up after 30 s so that no single workflow can hold a worker. (4) Every ready node becomes&#10;a Pod whose main container is wrapped by argoexec emissary as PID 1. (5) The executor never writes the Workflow. It creates or patches a&#10;WorkflowTaskResult named after the node ID, outside the write boundary, and the controller merges it on the next pass, which is what closes&#10;the loop. (6) persistUpdates compresses status.nodes and offloads it to SQL when it is still too large, so every reader outside the controller must&#10;hydrate first. Commit not determined: this copy of the repository carries no .git directory. The init-less pod layout, a single supervisor&#10;container in place of init and wait, is an opt-in beta and is not drawn." style="text;html=1;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;fontColor=#5f6368;fontFamily=Times New Roman;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="36" y="946" width="976" height="150" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
